WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.690
Unsafe code needs more than a one-time
glance.

2
00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:06.588
The important question is not just whether
the code looked reasonable during the

3
00:00:06.668 --> 00:00:07.342
first review.

4
00:00:07.422 --> 00:00:11.296
The question is whether the team has
ongoing evidence that the safety assumptions

5
00:00:11.376 --> 00:00:14.801
still hold as the code changes, platforms
change, dependencies update,

6
00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.285
and surrounding APIs evolve.

7
00:00:16.365 --> 00:00:19.846
Tests should cover expected behavior,
boundary values, invalid inputs,

8
00:00:19.926 --> 00:00:23.464
empty inputs, unusual sizes, concurrency
expectations, and error paths.

9
00:00:23.544 --> 00:00:27.530
Boundary tests are especially important
because unsafe code often exists where the

10
00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.698
program crosses from one representation or
system into another.

11
00:00:30.778 --> 00:00:34.316
Regression tests help preserve assumptions
after a bug or review finding.

12
00:00:34.396 --> 00:00:38.046
Review should focus on safety contracts,
invariants, pointer assumptions,

13
00:00:38.126 --> 00:00:41.944
FFI assumptions, layout assumptions,
thread behavior, public API guarantees,

14
00:00:42.024 --> 00:00:43.091
and maintenance risk.

15
00:00:43.171 --> 00:00:46.989
A reviewer should be able to connect each
unsafe operation to a documented reason

16
00:00:47.069 --> 00:00:49.259
and to evidence that the reason still
applies.

17
00:00:49.339 --> 00:00:52.595
Tooling can support review through
formatting, linting, CI checks,

18
00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:56.437
dependency review, and specialized
runtime-checking concepts such as Miri or

19
00:00:56.517 --> 00:00:57.135
sanitizers.

20
00:00:57.215 --> 00:01:00.303
Not every tool is available or appropriate
in every environment,

21
00:01:00.383 --> 00:01:04.201
but teams should understand the tool
categories and use the ones that match their

22
00:01:04.281 --> 00:01:04.955
risk profile.

23
00:01:05.035 --> 00:01:09.022
Maintenance matters because a safe
abstraction can become unsafe if future changes

24
00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.572
violate an assumption nobody documented or
tested.

25
00:01:11.652 --> 00:01:14.684
Updating safety documentation is part of
changing unsafe code.

26
00:01:14.764 --> 00:01:18.189
If a refactor changes ownership, layout,
lifetime, or thread behavior,

27
00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:20.740
the safety contract and tests should
change with it.
